    "case_id": "19",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old woman with a history of adenocarcinoma of the rectum and genital cancer underwent abdominoperineal resection with a permanent left ostomy and total hysterectomy at the age of 37. Nine years later, she was found to have colonic polyps with malignant transformation and was treated by surgical resection (colotomy and polypectomy). In 1993 and 1997, she had benign colonic polyps which were excised endoscopically. In March and June 2003, she was referred to our surgical outpatient department with subcutaneous lesions, first on the left side of the abdominal wall and then on the right buttock.\n\nThe lesions were excised under local anesthesia and were reported to be sebaceous adenomas.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
